Kernel 3 — What it actually is
Name (clean)

Kernel 3 — Frontend Shell, Cave View, and Policy-Driven Visibility

What this kernel must prove

Not beauty.

Not flexibility.

Just truth:

1. Same session, different sight
actor sees fire
audience does not
2. Policy changes reality
toggle actors_can_reveal
actor reveals fire
audience now sees it
3. System is no longer terminal-driven
no wscat
no curl for core interaction
real browser client
What the next agent must build
1. Frontend shell (minimal)
login-aware
session-aware
route to the-cave
role-aware UI

No styling obsession.

2. Cave page
Must include:
Stage viewport (PixiJS inside)
render a single image (fire asset)
layered text overlay (speech)
simple reaction indicator
Controls (role-based)

Actor / Director:

speak input
reveal fire toggle (respects policy)

Audience:

reaction buttons only
no reveal control
3. Policy hook (minimal)

One production-level config:

actors_can_reveal = true/false

Behavior:

if false → only director can reveal
if true → actor can reveal

No UI editor needed yet. Hardcode or simple config fetch is fine.

4. Visibility system (first real proof)

Backend already knows truth.

Frontend must enforce:

IF role == audience AND fire_visible == false
  → do not render fire
ELSE
  → render fire

That’s the entire illusion layer v1.

5. Renderer usage (important constraint)

Use PixiJS, but:

no scene system
no layout engine
no drag/drop
no complex abstraction

Just:

draw image
draw text
update on events
What must NOT be built

Keep the next agent from wandering.

Do NOT build:

drag-and-drop
workshop UI polish
map redesign
multiple venues
black box systems
asset editor tools
animation systems
camera systems
3D anything
Success criteria (very clear)

Kernel 3 passes when:

Two users log in (actor + audience)
Both enter the-cave
Actor sees fire immediately
Audience does not
Actor clicks “Reveal Fire”
Audience now sees the same fire
Actor speaks → audience sees text
Audience reacts → actor sees reaction